  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nGenerally low impact weather is expected through the end of the\nweek and this weekend across eastern Oklahoma and northwest\nArkansas. Surface ridging remains nearby the local region as mid\nlevel high pressure builds back eastward and flattens out over\nmuch of the southern states through the middle part of the week.\nThe result will be near persistent, dry conditions, with a slight\nwarming trend each day.\n\nBy the end of the week and into the weekend, guidance remains\nconsistent in lifting a tropical low northward out of the Gulf,\ntracking up the Mississippi Valley before lifting northeastward\nthrough the Ohio Valley. This track would keep much in the way of\nimpactful weather and rain chances east of the area, though a few\nshowers could creep into western Arkansas Saturday night into\nSunday morning. The primary response felt across our forecast area\nwill be an increase in southerly to southeasterly winds in \nresponse to the tightening gradient as the low lifts northward \nacross eastern Arkansas. Fire weather concerns could increase \nduring this timeframe with the gustier surface winds and continued\ndrought conditions across the region. A storm system and \nassociated cold front could approach the region toward the middle \npart of next week, maintaining gusty winds each afternoon ahead of\nthe front. Rain chances begin to increase just outside the range \nof this forecast package as the front finally moves through the \nforecast area the latter half of next week.\n\nBowlan",
